  "tour": {
    "title": "Your Body, Resurrection, and the New Creation",
    "intro": "This tour separates two ideas that are often blended together: what may be true for a believer immediately after death, and the New Testament’s final hope of resurrection. It begins with the vocabulary of resurrection, then follows Paul’s body-language and the Bible’s picture of heaven and earth finally reunited.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:6a253c6-com-651c11dc-e7c9-3e9c-8d28-dfdec7d811d5",
        "startSec": 670,
        "endSec": 840,
        "why": "Start here to establish the central distinction you are asking about. N. T. Wright says plainly that “resurrection is not about somebody going into a glorious angelic state” and that the word does not mean “going to heaven”; he then connects Jesus’ identifiable, wounded risen body with the question of what God will do with ours.",
        "episodeTitle": "#164 Easter questions on the resurrection of Jesus",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR6878813537.mp3?updated=1749655811",
        "durationSec": 2012,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:5c0c2da1-com-b64a4f2b-7912-37f8-814f-881acb5b8ead",
        "startSec": 490,
        "endSec": 564,
        "why": "Now hear the important qualification about the time between death and resurrection. Wright says that a believer is “safe with Jesus,” describes the dead as “waiting for resurrection,” and treats paradise and the place Jesus prepares as an interim rest rather than the final destination. That keeps “with Christ now” from being confused with the New Testament’s ultimate bodily hope.",
        "episodeTitle": "#136 Talking to kids about New Creation & responding to William Lane Craig",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR1804642062.mp3?updated=1749724438",
        "durationSec": 1710,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:resurrection-way-life-part-4-acorns-fire",
        "startSec": 954,
        "endSec": 1153,
        "why": "This gives you Paul’s answer to “what kind of body?” through the seed image: the body is “planted perishable” but “raised imperishable,” related to what was sown yet transformed. The speaker explicitly rejects the idea that the biblical future is to “float away as then a spirit,” and explains the contrast between present “flesh and blood” and an imperishable existence in God’s kingdom.",
        "episodeTitle": "Acorns of Fire",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/e5d85dd4-a616-4860-846c-fc4d57e11252/episodes/c2774234-47e5-4281-a3bc-72c252ed1861/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=e5d85dd4-a616-4860-846c-fc4d57e11252&awEpisodeId=c2774234-47e5-4281-a3bc-72c252ed1861",
        "durationSec": 2953,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:8380ffa3-com-b72975ac-389f-3ffa-84fb-478fb64f2873",
        "startSec": 602,
        "endSec": 846,
        "why": "Finish by listening directly through the logic of 1 Corinthians 15:35–39. This exposition frames resurrection as central and guaranteed by Jesus’ resurrection, then walks through Paul’s seed and “different kinds of bodies” analogies. It helps you hear “spiritual body” as a body fitted for resurrection life, not as a denial of embodiment.",
        "episodeTitle": "1 Corinthians 15:29-58",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/d8ngn3/1_corinthians_1529-589yhwk.mp3",
        "durationSec": 2010,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips present the New Testament’s sequence as: death, being with Christ, and finally resurrection at Christ’s coming. The final hope is not escape from creation but God’s renewal of embodied life—“heaven and earth together,” as Wright puts it—following the pattern already begun in Jesus.",
    "totalSeconds": 687
  },
